{"id":"a1c65331-0906-4a5c-aad2-cd3cfdaa7bda","object":"chat.completion","created":1784886370,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    # Extract payload\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n\\n    # Build node map and children lists\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        node_by_id[n['id']] = n\\n\\n    # Find root\\n    roots = [n for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None]\\n    if len(roots) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Must have exactly one root\\\")\\n    root = roots[0]\\n\\n    # Verify all required symbols are present\\n    all_symbols = {n['symbol'] for n in nodes}\\n    req_sym_set = set(required_symbols)\\n    missing = req_sym_set - all_symbols\\n    if missing:\\n        raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbols missing: {missing}\\\")\\n\\n    # Build children mapping\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n\\n    # Initial retained set\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n\\n    # Collect candidates: removable nodes, not root, not a required symbol\\n    candidates = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if (n['removable'] and n['id'] != root['id']\\n                and n['symbol'] not in req_sym_set):\\n            candidates.append(n)\\n\\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n\\n    for n in candidates:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Cannot remove if it has any retained children\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Check that all required symbols would still be present\\n        temp_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        all_required_ok = True\\n        for sym in req_sym_set:\\n            if not any(node_by_id[n2]['symbol'] == sym for n2 in temp_retained):\\n                all_required_ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not all_required_ok:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Perform removal\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n\\n    # Sort retained ids\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` for task SE-PE-01. The task is about grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree. The payload contains a list of nodes forming a tree (each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility). Also required_symbols list. The objective: reduce the tree by removing removable nodes while preserving required symbols, and following some ordering: consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes, never remove root, required symbol nodes, or nodes with retained children. Tentatively remove only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as tie break at equal depth. Return removed_node_ids (in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids sorted, removed_node_ids in removal order.\n\nWe need to implement algorithm: Build tree from nodes. Identify removable nodes. Sort candidates: first by depth descending (deeper first), then by node id as tie break (string? probably lexicographically). Then iterate over sorted candidates: for each node, check if it can be removed: not root, not required symbol, not a node that has retained children (children that are not removed yet and also not pending removal?), and after removal all required symbols still present (i.e., required symbols appear among remaining nodes). If all conditions met, remove it (add to removed list), and update state. Continue until no more removable nodes can be removed? The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests we only remove if condition holds, and we might stop when no more can be removed. But also \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we sort all candidates initially, then iterate once? But after removing some nodes, previously not removable might become removable? E.g., removing a node might make its parent have no retained children, but parent may be removable if it wasn't before? The description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node had retained children, it cannot be removed until children are removed. That suggests a dynamic process: after removing children, parent may become removable. So we need to repeatedly process candidates until no more removals. The ordering: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That implies we should always try deeper nodes first. So we need a loop: while there are changes, recompute the set of removable nodes that are candidates? Or maintain a priority queue (max depth, then ID). Since depths are known and fixed, we can process nodes in decreasing depth order, and after each removal, we don't need to reprocess shallower nodes immediately because we already processed them? But if we process deeper nodes first, after removing a child, the parent might become eligible. However the parent is shallower. The algorithm should \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we should always prioritize deeper nodes. So we can maintain a sorted list of removable nodes by (depth descending, id ascending). Then iterate through this list, but after each removal, we need to update the eligibility of other nodes (especially the parent of the removed node) and also maybe siblings? The parent's children count decreases, but if child is removed, parent might lose all retained children. So parent might become removable. The parent is shallower, so it should be considered after all deeper nodes are processed. Since we are iterating from deepest to shallowest, if we remove a child that is the last retained child of parent, then parent becomes a candidate. However, we already passed the parent's depth earlier? Actually we start with deepest nodes. If parent depth=1, child depth=2. When we process all depth-2 nodes, we might remove some. After finishing depth-2, we move to depth-1. At that point, the parent might now have no retained children, so it becomes removable. That will be considered when we process depth-1 nodes. So a single pass over sorted list (by depth descending) might work if we recompute the list after each removal? But we need to ensure we don't miss a node that becomes removable after we have already passed its depth level. Since we process depth levels from max to min, and after finishing a depth, no deeper nodes remain, but removal of a node at depth d might affect nodes at depth d-1 (or same depth? siblings). Siblings at same depth: removal of a sibling doesn't affect the other sibling's removability (unless parent retained children condition? Sibling removal may affect parent but not the other sibling directly). So after removing a node at depth d, the parent at depth d-1 might become eligible. But we have not yet processed depth d-1 if we are still at depth d? Actually we process all nodes at depth d before moving to d-1. So if we remove a node at depth d, we should, after the removal, still continue processing the remaining nodes at depth d (since we are iterating within the same depth level). But the parent is at depth d-1, which is not yet processed. So after finishing depth d, we will process depth d-1, and at that point the parent might be removable. That is fine. However, what about nodes at the same depth that become eligible due to removal of another node? For example, two siblings that are both removable but their parent is not removable because it has retained children. If one sibling is removed, the parent still has the other sibling, so parent still not removable. The other sibling remains removable. But its removability condition might change if its own children were removed? That's deeper nodes. So same depth siblings' removability is independent of each other except through parent. So a single pass from deepest to shallowest, with each depth level processed in ID order, seems sufficient. But after removing a node, we need to check its children (deeper) are already removed? Actually if a node is removed, its children must have been removed first (since we never remove a node with retained children). So children should already be removed. So we don't create new opportunities for deeper nodes. Only for the parent. So single pass from deepest to shallowest works.\n\nHowever, we must ensure that we re-evaluate the parent's removability after all its children that are candidates have been considered. Since we process depth descending, all children (depth > parent) are processed before parent. So when we get to parent, the children that could be removed are already removed (if they satisfied conditions). So the parent's retained children count is final. So we can decide parent's removability at that point. Thus one pass over all nodes sorted by (depth descending, id ascending) is sufficient, provided we only consider nodes that are currently retained and removable. But we need to start with all removable nodes? But some may not be removable initially because they have retained children. However, we want to consider them only after their children are removed. By processing depth descending, we first process deep nodes that have no children (leaf nodes). For leaf nodes, if removable, we can decide to remove them if conditions hold. Then as we move up, we process nodes that might have had children that were removed, so they might become eligible. So we need to dynamically check at each node if it can be removed based on current state. The algorithm:\n\n1. Build map id->node dict. Build children list per parent. Determine root.\n2. Build set of required symbols. Determine required symbol nodes: nodes whose symbol in required_symbols. But careful: required_symbols are symbols, not node IDs. So we need to ensure that after removals, at least one node with each required symbol remains. So we need to track which nodes are still present and what symbols they give.\n3. Start with set of current retained nodes = all nodes. For each node, keep track if it's removed.\n4. Build list of all removable nodes (where removable=True and not root and symbol not in required_symbols? Actually the condition \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" means nodes with symbol in required_symbols? But required_symbols might be symbols that must appear at least once. It does not say that a node with required symbol cannot be removed if there is another node with same symbol. However, constraint: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This implies that you can remove a node with a required symbol as long as that symbol still appears elsewhere. But \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" This says \"a required symbol\" meaning the node's symbol is required. That seems absolute: never remove a node that is a required symbol. However, the next sentence says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This is a contradiction if required symbol nodes cannot be removed. I think the intended meaning: never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols? But then the second condition would be redundant. Let's re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That appears to list three categories that cannot be removed regardless. Then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That is an additional condition that applies even to nodes not in those categories. So \"a required symbol\" likely means a node whose symbol is required. So such nodes are absolutely protected. But then why check that all required symbols remain represented? Because if a required symbol node is protected, it cannot be removed, so it's always represented. But there could be multiple nodes with same symbol; some might be removable. The second condition ensures that after removal, the required symbol still appears somewhere. So we must protect all nodes that have a required symbol? That contradicts \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". That means any node with a required symbol cannot be removed. So that is absolute. So we can simply filter out those nodes from removable set.\n\nThus: removable nodes = nodes where removable=True and id != root_id and symbol not in required_symbols_set. Then also they must not have retained children. But we can check dynamically.\n\nAlgorithm steps:\n- Parse payload: nodes list, required_symbols list.\n- Build dictionary: node_by_id, children_by_parent.\n- Determine root: node with parent None.\n- required_sym_set = set(required_symbols)\n- Initialize retained = set(all node ids).\n- Initialize removed = [] (ordered).\n- Get list of all nodes that are removable in principle: candidates = [n for n in nodes if n['removable'] and n['id'] != root['id'] and n['symbol'] not in required_sym_set]\n- Sort candidates by (-depth, id) (id as string compare).\n- For each node in sorted_candidates:\n    - If node['id'] not in retained: continue (already removed)\n    - Check if node has any retained children (i.e., any child whose id is in retained). If any, skip.\n    - Check if after removing this node, all required symbols still have at least one node with that symbol among retained nodes (excluding this node). Compute: for each sym in required_sym_set, check if there exists any retained node with that symbol (other than this node if it has that symbol). Since we already excluded nodes with required symbols from being removable, this condition automatically holds? Actually if we exclude nodes with required symbols from candidates, then removing this node won't remove a required symbol. But there might be a case where the node's symbol is not required, but the only node with a required symbol is also being removed? No, we are not removing that. So condition is automatically satisfied if we excluded required symbol nodes. But what if there is only one node with a required symbol and it is not removable (protected), so it remains. So condition holds. However, there may be multiple nodes with a required symbol; we might remove all but one. That's fine as long as one remains. But we are not removing required symbol nodes, so at least one remains. So condition is redundant given the absolute protection. But to be safe, we can still check. The description says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" - that is absolute. So we can skip the check. But the phrase \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" might apply to nodes that are not themselves required but whose removal could remove the last instance of a required symbol? That can't happen if required symbols are only on nodes that are protected. Unless required symbols can appear on removable nodes? The phrase \"a required symbol\" might be ambiguous: it could mean the node whose symbol is required. So we protect those nodes. Then the second condition is already ensured. I'll include the check for completeness.\n\n- If all conditions met, remove the node: move from retained to removed, add to removed list.\n- Continue.\n- After loop, build retained_node_ids = sorted list of retained set.\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}.\n\nImportant: we must not mutate case. So copy data.\n\nNow, about \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The output schema requires both arrays. retained_node_ids must be sorted (presumably lexicographically). removed_node_ids in order of removal.\n\nAlso, \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sorting candidates by depth descending, id ascending.\n\nEdge cases: If a node is removable but has retained children, skip. After removing children, parent may become eligible. Since we process in depth descending, children processed before parent. So when we get to parent, children are already removed (if they were removable and conditions met). But what if a child was not removable? Then parent still has retained child -> skip parent. That's fine.\n\nAlso, we must ensure we only consider candidates that are still retained. So in loop, skip if already removed.\n\nNow, implement solve(case). case is the payload object. It has nodes list, required_symbols list, maybe _case_nonce but ignore.\n\nReturn dict with \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\".\n\nWe need to handle invalid cases? Raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid. But not sure what constitutes infeasible. Possibly if required symbol not present at all? But we can just return with all nodes retained? The spec says to raise ValueError if infeasible. For example, if required_symbols contain symbol not present in any node, then it's impossible to preserve. We should raise ValueError. Also if root not found, etc.\n\nThus implement validation:\n- Ensure nodes list non-empty.\n- Ensure root exists (parent None).\n- Ensure all required symbols appear in at least one node (even if protected, but they might not be removable). Actually they need to be represented, so if any required symbol is missing, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso, ensure node IDs unique, parent references valid, etc. Probably not needed but we can check.\n\nNow produce implementation as Python string.\n\nWe need to output JSON with single key \"implementation\" containing the Python code as a string (escaped). The code must be valid Python, using only standard library.\n\nWrite solve(case) function.\n\nTesting with the given fixtures? Not required but we can reason.\n\nLet's implement step by step.\n\nI'll produce the code.\n\nPotential issue: The nodes list may not be sorted by depth. But we compute depth from node field, so ok.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    # Build maps\n    node_by_id = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        node_by_id[n['id']] = n\n    # Check root\n    roots = [n for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None]\n    if len(roots) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Must have exactly one root\")\n    root = roots[0]\n    # Check required symbols present\n    all_symbols = set(n['symbol'] for n in nodes)\n    required_sym_set = set(required_symbols)\n    missing = required_sym_set - all_symbols\n    if missing:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Required symbols missing: {missing}\")\n    # Build children sets\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n['parent']\n        if p is not None:\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\n    # Initial retained set\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\n    removed = []\n    # Prepare candidates: all removable nodes except root and required symbols\n    candidates = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['removable'] and n['id'] != root['id'] and n['symbol'] not in required_sym_set:\n            candidates.append(n)\n    # Sort by depth descending, id ascending\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\n    # Process\n    for n in candidates:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Check retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check if removal would lose a required symbol (should not happen due to protection, but check)\n        # For each required symbol, ensure there is at least one retained node (excluding this one) with that symbol.\n        # Since n's symbol is not required, no issue.\n        # However, what if n's symbol is not required but removing it could affect depth? No.\n        # Still check for correctness.\n        # Actually if we protect required symbols, no need. But we can do:\n        temp_retained = retained - {nid}\n        ok = True\n        for sym in required_sym_set:\n            if not any(node_by_id[nid2]['symbol'] == sym for nid2 in temp_retained):\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if not ok:\n            continue\n        # Remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed.append(nid)\n    # Sort retained\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nThat's the core. However, we need to consider that after removing nodes, the condition \"removable\" might need to be re-evaluated for nodes that are not in original candidates (e.g., nodes that were not removable because they had retained children but then children removed). But we included all nodes that are removable in principle (removable=True and not root and not required). Even if they have retained children initially, they are in candidates list. When we process them later (since depth descending), their children should have been processed earlier. But if a node has retained children that are not removable (e.g., children with required symbol), then the parent cannot be removed. That's fine; we skip and don't remove. Could there be a case where a node's child is removed, making parent eligible, but parent was in candidates list and we already passed its depth? Since depth of parent is less than children, we process children first (deeper). Then when we reach parent (shallower), the child is removed, so parent's retained children condition becomes false (no retained children), so parent can be removed at that point. That's fine. So our single pass works.\n\nBut what about a node that is initially not even a candidate because removable=False? We can never remove it.\n\nOne nuance: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That includes nodes that have children that are also in the process of being removed? Since we never remove a node until after its children are removed (because children are deeper and processed earlier), retained children set does not include nodes already removed. So condition works.\n\nThus algorithm seems correct.\n\nNow produce final JSON output.\n\nWe must ensure the Python code is a single string, properly escaped. We'll use json.dumps to escape.\n\nWe'll write the code as a multiline string and then use json.dumps on the dict {\"implementation\": code}.\n\nBut careful: The submission envelope says \"Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\" So we need to produce valid JSON with the implementation key.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n\nWe'll use triple quotes for the multiline string in Python to avoid many escapes, but final output must be JSON. So we'll write a Python script that builds the dict and prints json.dumps.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer directly.\n\nNow write the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":4933,"total_tokens":7102,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4310},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}